<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Mongol World</h1><p class="path-tagline">Follow Genghis Khan's empire from the steppe to the rival khanates and Timur's heirs.</p><p class="path-intro">In 1206 a kurultai on the Mongolian steppe proclaimed Temüjin Genghis Khan, and his armies built the largest contiguous land empire in history. Within two generations his sons and grandsons ruled from Sarai on the Volga, from Persia and Iraq, from the Silk Road cities of Central Asia and from Dadu in China, and the succession war of 1260–1264 split the empire into khanates. This path follows each of them: the Golden Horde and the Rus' princes, the Ilkhanate that ended the Abbasid Caliphate, the Chagatai realm that produced Timur, and Kublai's Yuan Dynasty, closing with the Timurid Empire, whose courts at Samarkand and Herat lasted until 1507.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Navigator</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Work · 13th century</span><span class="path-step-name">Secret History of the Mongols</span><span class="path-step-why">The oldest surviving work of Mongolian literature tells of Temüjin's youth and his struggles with the Tatars and Merkits before his rise to power.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Secret History of the Mongols is the oldest surviving work of Mongolian literature, written in the 13th century, probably soon after the death of Genghis Khan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mongol-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1206 CE – 1368 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mongol Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Proclaimed Genghis Khan at a kurultai in 1206, Temüjin built the largest contiguous land empire in history, which split into rival khanates after 1260.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Mongol Empire was founded by Genghis Khan in 1206 and became the largest contiguous land empire in history, stretching from Korea to eastern Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1205–1255</span><span class="path-step-name">Batu Khan</span><span class="path-step-why">A grandson of Genghis Khan, Batu led the western campaign of 1236 to 1242 into Rus', Poland and Hungary, then ruled the western steppe from Sarai.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Batu Khan was a grandson of Genghis Khan and founder of the Golden Horde.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/golden-horde"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mengu-timur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1242 CE – 1502 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Golden Horde</span><span class="path-step-why">From Sarai on the Volga, Jochi's descendants took tribute from the Rus' princes for over two centuries, until the Great Stand on the Ugra in 1480 ended Moscow's tribute.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Golden Horde was the Mongol khanate in the western steppe, founded by descendants of Genghis Khan's son Jochi.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1217–1265</span><span class="path-step-name">Hulagu Khan</span><span class="path-step-why">Another grandson led the Mongol campaigns into south-western Asia in the 1250s, sacking Baghdad in 1258 and ending the Abbasid Caliphate.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Hulagu Khan was a Mongol prince, a grandson of Genghis Khan, who led the Mongol campaigns into south-western Asia in the 1250s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ilkhanate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-soltaniyeh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1256 CE – 1357 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ilkhanate</span><span class="path-step-why">Hulagu's khanate in Persia and Iraq became a Persianised Islamic state under Ghazan, who converted in 1295 and began sweeping reforms with Rashid al-Din.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Ilkhanate was a Mongol state founded by Hulagu Khan in the mid-thirteenth century, ruling Iran, Iraq and neighbouring lands.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/chagatai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaidu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1225 CE – 1500 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Chagatai Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Genghis Khan allotted Central Asia's steppe and Silk Road cities to his son Chagatai; the khanate turned Muslim and Turkic and lost Transoxiana to Timur by 1370.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Chagatai Khanate was the Mongol successor state in Central Asia ruled by the descendants of Chagatai Khan from the thirteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1215–1294</span><span class="path-step-name">Kublai Khan</span><span class="path-step-why">Great Khan and founder of the Yuan Dynasty, Kublai completed the conquest of the Song in 1279, and his disputed succession helped break the empire apart.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Kublai Khan was a grandson of Genghis Khan who became Great Khan of the Mongol Empire and founded the Yuan Dynasty in China.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1271 CE – 1368 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Yuan Dynasty</span><span class="path-step-why">Ruling China from Dadu, the Yuan linked it to Persia and Europe across the Mongol network until floods, inflation and the Red Turbans brought it down in 1368.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol-ruled dynasty of China, proclaimed by Kublai Khan in 1271, which completed the conquest of the Southern Song in 1279.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/timurid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarkand.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1363 CE – 1507 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Timurid Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Timur, produced by the Chagatai realm, conquered from Anatolia to the borders of China, and his heirs made Samarkand and Herat centres of painting, poetry and astronomy.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Timurid Empire was the state founded by Timur in the late fourteenth century, centred on Transoxiana, Iran and Afghanistan, with capitals at Samarkand and later Herat.</span></span></a></li></ol>
